  "limitations": [
    "API-09 still owns the process-wide application/problem+json vocabulary, browser Origin and CSRF controls, per-source admission rate limiting, and global timeout and retry policy. API-05 has route-local fixed errors, a five-second transport wait, signature freshness bounds, and compiled per-template runtime limits, but does not claim the complete API-09 boundary.",
    "API-05 authenticates one registered HMAC-SHA256 source definition backed by an opaque environment secret reference and a static in-process registry. Secret provisioning, rotation orchestration, multiple provider-specific signature schemes, and operational source administration remain deployment work rather than capabilities claimed by this requirement.",
    "The webhook receipt acknowledges durable WorkItem and primary Run admission only; it does not claim inline workflow execution or external delivery. API-07 still owns the run-detail resource behind each runUrl, and later orchestration, approval, and connector requirements own execution after the received transition.",
    "DEL-04 still owns migrations and repeatable insert-only seed bootstrap. API-05 proves ORM constraints, foreign keys, hydration validation, and real SQLite persistence using test-created metadata. PostgreSQL schema portability is source-inspected but unexecuted: the generic optional driver test may skip and is not evidence of API-05 DDL or runtime behavior, and this gate claims neither an Alembic migration nor a live PostgreSQL database.",
    "DEL-05 still owns runnable default startup and service composition. The route is installed and fails closed without an asynchronous executor, while the API and database integration witnesses inject the real verifier, source registry, compiled resolver, digest key, repositories, and webhook unit of work; they do not claim the default process constructs or configures those dependencies yet.",
    "The SQLite webhook unit of work takes write intent before source-event lookup and serializes the default-timeout race; aggressive lock timeouts can exhaust bounded retries with a safe unavailable result. PostgreSQL uses deterministic configuration-row locks and re-reads the receipt after a waiter acquires them. A focused service test covers that branch, but this local gate has no live PostgreSQL concurrency run.",
    "New-source resolution currently locks the fixed 43-row configuration set to obtain one coherent fan-out snapshot. That bounded v1 design favors correctness over cross-source write concurrency; a future indexed trigger projection can narrow the lock set without changing the receipt contract."
  ]
